Clarity 7: a public or read-only function may be defined under a reserved name that was free in an earlier Clarity version, but only to implement a matching method of an implemented trait deployed while the name was still free. This keeps legacy traits whose method names later became natives (e.g. `slice?`, `stacks-block-height`) implementable at the latest Clarity version; a bare reference inside the contract still resolves to the native, and from outside the function is reachable only by literal-name lookup (`contract-call?` and trait dispatch); a function named after a native keyword is additionally callable in head position inside the contract, since no native function has that name. The rule is checked at analysis and repeated by the VM at initialization; an unmatched definition fails with the same `NameAlreadyUsed` error as in earlier versions.
Clarity 7: `define-trait` now rejects method names that are currently reserved; only traits deployed before a name's reservation can make it implementable.
